{"authored" [{"question" "q-79330" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={e}^{-4x}$\n- $y={e}^{4x}$\n- $y=4{e}^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e0b7e6136daf0695554759945e9d6facd93b6e14d84e75100dd6a2c1a0f1f02" "basis" 1388 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79330" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{-4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & \\overset{?}{=}4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 8{e}^{-4x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{4x} & =4{e}^{4x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={e}^{4x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=4{e}^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4(4{e}^{x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}16{e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 12{e}^{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=4{e}^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e0b7e6136daf0695554759945e9d6facd93b6e14d84e75100dd6a2c1a0f1f02" "basis" 1388 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79330" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ca52bc93660ff3ec41ee8d6300d81e7d44460f4c611f993b95adf8805394af7" "basis" 1388 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79330" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e0b7e6136daf0695554759945e9d6facd93b6e14d84e75100dd6a2c1a0f1f02" "basis" 1388 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79330" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e0b7e6136daf0695554759945e9d6facd93b6e14d84e75100dd6a2c1a0f1f02" "basis" 1388 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/edb-import/verificat