grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e0b7e6136daf0695554759945e9d6facd93b6e14d84e75100dd6a2c1a0f1f02" "basis" 1388 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{5}$\n- $y={x}^{10}$\n- $y=5{x}^{4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14054574/state.json" "file_sha256" "f302c4b320e5199e705316187e43a053ec22cb7769bdfb0fcc1dbdd6a96efbcb" "basis" 1814 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14054574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{5},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{5})}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & =5{x}^{4}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{5}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{10},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=10{x}^{9}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{10})}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}5{x}^{9} \\\\ 5{x}^{9} & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{10}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=5{x}^{4},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{3}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5(5{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}25{x}^{3} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 5{x}^{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=5{x}^{4}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14054574/state.json" "file_sha256" "f302c4b320e5199e705316187e43a053ec22cb7769bdfb0fcc1dbdd6a96efbcb" "basis" 1814 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14054574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14054574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8ec22a625be564740b161a2a66fd3e38dacf46fcd5e1aae79ce3478fd7857aa9" "basis" 1814 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14054574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14054574/state.json" "file_sha256" "f302c4b320e5199e705316187e43a053ec22cb7769bdfb0fcc1dbdd6a96efbcb" "basis" 1814 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14054574/ed